truth.correction:
Keep the gap: the race trunk motion is fixed and no per-horse control scales it. Restate the real-horse expectation in three parts:

1. The lumbosacral joint is the most mobile flexion-extension joint of the thoracolumbar spine (Townsend et al. 1983). But the horse back as a whole is stiff ("dorsostable"), with small ranges:
   - about 16° of total thoracolumbar flexion-extension at canter;
   - up to about 8.6° at L5-S3 (Faber et al. 2001);
   - about 10° of global back angle (Hatrisse et al. 2023);
   - 2-8° of trunk pitch (Dunbar et al. 2008).
2. "Uses the whole body" (Inari One, from his jockey) is a subjective impression. It is best shown through:
   - longer limb reach and sweep, meaning stride length relative to height and more protraction and retraction;
   - larger head and neck excursion;
   - with back and pelvis motion changing only a little.
3. The model's race preset already moves more than real horses: 18° peak-to-peak hips-on-spine gather (22.5° pelvis line in world space) and 7.6° withers-croup pitch.

So run.bodyAction should:
- act mainly on stance length (reach) and neck/head pump;
- scale spine pitch and lumbosacral gather only in a narrow band of about 0.8-1.1;
- ideally sit on a race base value lowered toward about 10-12° peak-to-peak of lumbosacral gather, with the bend moved to the true lumbosacral position.

It should not multiply the trunk motion up to 1.4×. The Townsend & Leach 1984 citation backs morphology-mobility only; cite Townsend, Leach & Fretz 1983 for the claim that the lumbosacral joint is the most mobile.
